📌SystemVerilog Testbench — README Description 🚀
♦️Overview 📋
This testbench verifies the 4-bit ALU using a layered, class-based SystemVerilog architecture 🏗️
Follows the Generator → Driver → Monitor → Scoreboard verification methodology 🔄
Automatically generates random test cases, applies them to the DUT, captures results, and self-checks pass/fail against a reference model ✅
Communication between components is handled using mailboxes 📬 (data) and events ⏰ (timing synchronization)
Architecture Components 🧩
1. Interface (alui) 🔌
⚫Bundles all DUT signals (a, b, opcode, result, cout, zero_flag) into a single connection point
⚫Acts as the bridge between the class-based testbench and the DUT
2. Transaction (transaction) 📦
⚫Represents one test case as an object
⚫Contains randomized stimulus: a, b, opcode 🎲
⚫Also stores captured DUT response: result, cout (filled in later by the monitor)
⚫randc is used on opcode for cyclic randomization — ensures all opcode values are covered evenly before repeating
3. Generator 🎰
⚫Creates 15 random transaction objects
⚫Sends each one to the driver via a mailbox
⚫Represents the stimulus-generation stage of verification
4. Driver 🚗
⚫Receives transactions from the generator's mailbox
⚫Drives a, b, opcode onto the DUT through the virtual interface
⚫Waits #2 time units for the DUT's combinational logic to settle
⚫Fires an event (drv_done) 🔔 to signal the monitor that new, stable values are ready to be sampled
5. Monitor 👀
⚫Waits for the driver's drv_done event before doing anything (passive observer)
⚫Once triggered, captures the DUT's actual signals: a, b, opcode, result, cout
⚫Packages these into a transaction and forwards it to the scoreboard via a separate mailbox
6. Scoreboard 🧮
⚫Maintains a reference model (ref_model) — a software re-implementation of expected ALU behavior for every opcode
⚫Compares the DUT's actual output (from the monitor) against the reference model's expected output
⚫Tracks and reports PASS ✅ / FAIL ❌ for every transaction
⚫Maintains running pass_count and fail_count totals
💠Synchronization: Events vs Mailboxes ⚙️
Mechanism	Purpose
⬛Mailbox 📬	Transfers actual data (transaction objects) between components (Generator→Driver, Monitor→Scoreboard)
⬛Event (drv_done) ⏰	Synchronizes timing — ensures the monitor only samples the DUT right after the driver has applied inputs and the result has settled, avoiding race conditions
💠Top-Level Module (alu_tb) 🏛️
⚫Instantiates all four verification components (Generator, Driver, Monitor, Scoreboard)
⚫Instantiates the DUT (alu_4bit) and connects it via the interface
⚫Wires up mailboxes and events between components
⚫Uses fork...join_none to run Driver, Monitor, and Scoreboard concurrently in the background 🔀
⚫Calls the Generator directly (blocking) so the main thread waits until all 15 transactions are generated
⚫Adds delay padding (#50) before and after generation to allow the pipeline to fully process all transactions ⏳
⚫Prints a final test summary 📊 with total pass/fail counts
⚫Includes $dumpfile/$dumpvars for waveform generation (viewable in EPWave) 📈
💠Key SystemVerilog Concepts Demonstrated 🎓
⚫Class-based OOP verification (class, new(), inheritance-ready structure)
⚫Randomization (rand, randc, randomize()) 🎲
⚫Mailboxes for inter-process communication 📬
⚫Events for process synchronization ⏰
⚫Virtual interfaces to connect class-based components to hardware signals 🔌
⚫fork...join_none for concurrent process execution 🔀
⚫Reference-model-based self-checking (automated scoreboard) ✅
